File: PrimaryDecomposition.info, Node: topComponents, Prev: strategies for computing primary decomposition, Up: Top

topComponents -- compute top dimensional component of an ideal or module
************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        topComponents M
  * Inputs:
      * M, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal, or a *note module:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Module,,
  * Outputs:
      * an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal, or a *note module:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, the intersection of the primary components of
        the input with the greatest Krull dimension

Description
===========

The method used is that of Eisenbud-Huneke-Vasconcelos, in their 1993
Inventiones Mathematicae paper.

+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/32003[a..c];                                                   |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = intersect(ideal(a,b), ideal(b,c), ideal(c,a), ideal(a^2,b^3,c^4));|
|                                                                           |
|o2 : Ideal of R                                                            |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : topComponents I                                                       |
|                                                                           |
|o3 = ideal (b*c, a*c, a*b)                                                 |
|                                                                           |
|o3 : Ideal of R                                                            |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+

If $M$ is a module in a polynomial ring $R$, then the implementations of
topComponents and *note removeLowestDimension: removeLowestDimension, are based
on the following observations:

  * $codim Ext^d(M,R) \ge d$ for all $d$
  * If $P$ is an associated prime of $M$ of codimension $d := codim P > codim
    M$, then $codim Ext^d(M,R) = d$ and the annihilator of $Ext^d(M,R)$ is
    contained in $P$
  * If $codim Ext^d(M,R) = d$, then there really is an associated prime of
    codimension $d$.
  * If $M$ is $R/I$, then $topComponents(I) = ann Ext^c(R/I,R)$, where $c =
    codim I$

See also
========

  * *note removeLowestDimension: removeLowestDimension, -- remove components of
    lowest dimension
  * *note saturate: (Saturation)saturate, -- saturation of ideal or submodule
  * *note Ideal _colon_ Ideal: (Saturation)quotient_lpModule_cmModule_rp, --
    ideal or submodule quotient
  * *note radical: (MinimalPrimes)radical, -- the radical of an ideal

Ways to use topComponents:
==========================

  * "topComponents(Ideal)"
  * "topComponents(Module)"
  * "topComponents(Module,ZZ)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note topComponents: topComponents, is a *note method function:
(Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunction,.

